<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    I-11 — why this file permits cleartext, and what actually stops it.

    Ollama and LM Studio run on the user's own machine and speak plain HTTP.
    Android has refused cleartext by default since Android 9, so a perfectly
    valid endpoint typed into Settings was rejected by the platform before a
    byte moved, with an error that explained nothing to anyone.

    The narrow fix would be to permit cleartext only for private address ranges.
    Android does not offer it: `<domain>` matches host NAMES, and the current
    documentation has no IP-literal or CIDR form. The choice is genuinely
    all-or-nothing, so the narrowing lives in the app instead —
    `src/lib/network/localEndpointPolicy.ts` accepts cleartext only to an IP
    literal inside RFC 1918 or loopback, and refuses every host NAME however
    local it sounds, because a name is resolved later and can answer with a
    public address.

    TALOS is distributed. This flag is not weakening one developer's phone, it
    is weakening everyone's, so the app-level rule is a tested contract rather
    than a comment: see tests/unit/lib/localEndpointPolicy.test.ts.

    The platform is not out of the picture either. From Android 17, reaching a
    local network address requires the ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK runtime permission,
    which the user grants and can revoke at any time — an OS-level gate this
    file cannot override.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!--
                System CAs only. User-installed certificates are deliberately
                NOT trusted: a distributed app should not honour a certificate
                someone was talked into installing.
            -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
